# The native projects are committed, not ignored.
#
# `android/` and `ios/` are the build. They are checked in so this is an
# ordinary Android and Xcode project that anyone can clone and build without
# running an Expo command first, and so a change to native config is reviewable
# in a diff rather than hidden inside a generator.
#
# There is no `app.json` and nothing regenerates these. This block used to say
# to treat `expo prebuild` as a bootstrap and re-run it — running it today would
# destroy both native projects, which is what `mobile/README.md` and the root
# README both say in as many words. Edit the native files directly.
#
# Only build output is ignored.
/android/build/
/android/app/build/
/android/.gradle/
/android/.idea/
/android/local.properties
/ios/build/
/ios/Pods/
/ios/*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
/ios/*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/

# Firebase's per-project config, kept out on purpose.
#
# Google calls these non-secret and they are, in the sense that the key inside is
# restricted to a package name and a signing certificate. What they are NOT is
# harmless in a PUBLIC repository: anyone who takes a copy can point crash
# reports and analytics at this project, and the first sign of it is a console
# full of events from a build nobody here made.
#
# So they are supplied rather than committed — by whoever is building, or by CI
# from a secret — which is the same bargain `android/app/build.gradle` already
# strikes over the upload keystore: "a keystore committed beside the code it
# signs is not a key". `build.gradle` applies the Google plugin only when the
# file is actually there, so a fork and a first clone still build.
/android/app/google-services.json
/ios/GoogleService-Info.plist
/ios/jojo/GoogleService-Info.plist
